# modules.cpp graphics module

# Status and boundary

This document specifies the implemented mm.gfx module. Its host tests cover
packing, clipping, rotation, region palettes, and writes through a recording
display. Visual output on physical panels still requires hardware
qualification. docs/modules-display.mdy owns display lifecycle and packed
transfer; mm.gfx owns caller-provided composition memory and drawing.

mm.gfx is an ordinary module with `use: mm.display`. It does not select a display,
allocate a framebuffer, or refresh a panel. A caller selects or receives a
Display, initializes it, writes one or more surfaces, and refreshes explicitly.

# Surface and colour

Opening fence
struct Surface {
    unsigned int width;
    unsigned int height;
    unsigned int bits_per_pixel;
    std::span<std::byte> pixels;
    constexpr std::size_t row_bytes() const;
    constexpr std::size_t size() const;
    constexpr bool valid() const;
};

struct Rgb565 { unsigned short value; };
constexpr Rgb565 rgb(unsigned char r, unsigned char g, unsigned char b);
struct Palette { Rgb565 ink; Rgb565 paper; };
struct Region { mm::display::Rectangle area; Palette palette; };
Closing fence

The caller owns `pixels`. Rows are packed tightly with no extra stride. At
one bit per pixel, the most significant bit is leftmost, one is white, and a
row ends at a byte boundary. At sixteen bits, pixels are RGB565 with the most
significant byte first. `maximum_side` is 1,048,576 pixels.

`row_bytes()` is `(width + 7) / 8` for one bit and `width * 2` for sixteen
bits. It returns zero for a zero or over-bound width or an unsupported depth.
`size()` returns `row_bytes() * height`, or zero for a zero or over-bound
height or an unrepresentable product. `valid()` requires nonzero `size()` and
at least that many bytes in `pixels`. These methods are safe on an invalid or
default-constructed Surface. Extra storage after the first `size()` bytes is
not part of the surface and is never accessed by drawing or writing.

`rgb` truncates eight-bit red and blue to five bits and green to six.
`rgb565_black`, `rgb565_white`, and `rgb565_red` name common values. For a
one-bit surface, drawing takes `mm::display::Color::Black` or `White`; Red
returns Unsupported. For a sixteen-bit surface, drawing takes Rgb565. A
colour overload used with the other depth returns BadArgument.

# Drawing

Opening fence
using mm::display::Color;

Status fill(Surface, Color);
Status fill(Surface, Rgb565);
Status pixel(Surface, int x, int y, Color);
Status pixel(Surface, int x, int y, Rgb565);
Status line(Surface, int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, Color);
Status line(Surface, int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, Rgb565);
Status rectangle(Surface, int x, int y, unsigned width, unsigned height,
                 Color);
Status rectangle(Surface, int x, int y, unsigned width, unsigned height,
                 Rgb565);
Status fill_rectangle(Surface, int x, int y, unsigned width,
                      unsigned height, Color);
Status fill_rectangle(Surface, int x, int y, unsigned width,
                      unsigned height, Rgb565);
Closing fence

Drawing coordinates are local to the surface and may be negative. Primitives
clip to its visible area; a shape wholly outside it or with zero extent is a
successful no-op. `rectangle` draws a one-pixel outline and
`fill_rectangle` paints its interior. Shapes overwrite in call order.

Lines include both endpoints and use Bresenham after clipping. The clipped
line may differ by a pixel from clipping an unclipped raster because its
starting phase changes. Clipping uses wide arithmetic for far-away origins;
the rasterizer operates on coordinates bounded by `maximum_side`.

Drawing a one-bit pixel preserves its neighbors and the unused padding bits
at the row's right edge. `fill` clears those padding bits. An invalid surface
returns BadArgument before any pixel is changed.

# Expansion and rotation

Opening fence
Status expand_row(std::span<const std::byte> packed_row,
                  unsigned int width, Palette,
                  std::span<std::byte> rgb565_row);
Status rotate(Surface source, unsigned int quarter_turns, Surface turned);
constexpr mm::display::Rectangle rotate(mm::display::Rectangle area,
                                         unsigned int frame_width,
                                         unsigned int frame_height,
                                         unsigned int quarter_turns);
Closing fence

`expand_row` consumes exactly `width` visible bits and produces `width * 2`
RGB565 bytes. Set bits take the palette's paper; cleared bits take its ink.
Input padding is ignored. It rejects short input or output spans.

The surface `rotate` turns a one-bit surface clockwise by quarter turns and
clears destination padding. Source and destination must be separate, valid
surfaces with the corresponding dimensions. The rectangle overload clips a
region to the original frame before turning it. A wholly outside or empty
rectangle returns an empty rectangle. Turning a region with its surface
preserves the pixels that receive its palette.

# Display writes and tiles

Opening fence
Status write(mm::display::Display&, Surface, unsigned display_x,
             unsigned display_y);
Status write(mm::display::Display&, Surface, unsigned display_x,
             unsigned display_y, Palette base,
             std::span<const Region> regions,
             std::span<std::byte> scratch);
Closing fence

Both overloads validate the surface and panel bounds before writing. The
first requires matching bit depth and passes exactly the first `size()` bytes
in one Display::write call. On a one-bit panel, `display_x` must be a multiple
of eight; surface width must be a multiple of eight unless the region reaches
the panel's right edge. This matches the SSD1680 window rule.

The second overload requires a one-bit surface and a sixteen-bit panel. It
expands one row at a time into caller scratch of at least `width * 2` bytes
and writes exactly that many bytes for each row. The first Region covering a
surface pixel supplies its palette; uncovered pixels use `base`. Regions are
clipped to the surface. A surface can be a full frame or a tile, and the
caller translates global shape coordinates when composing a tile.

Neither overload initializes or refreshes the display. A TFT can show each
write immediately; an ePaper provider makes it visible on refresh. The
interface has no readback. Repainting an old tile requires retained pixels
or replaying the shapes that cover it.

# Tests and limits

`tests/mm/gfx` uses a recording Display to check exact transferred bytes,
tile alignment, panel bounds, expansion palettes, and the absence of an
implicit refresh. It also checks surface sizes, packing, clipping, padding,
and rotation without hardware.

`apps/gfx-demo` draws a framed scene with every drawing primitive, rotates it
through four orientations, and writes it to either panel depth. On colour
panels it also draws an RGB565 swatch directly and writes that tile at the
panel's depth. Its build scripts cover Linux SDL, emulated e-paper, Pico
e-paper, and RP2350 LCD.

The one-bit surface has no red pigment plane, grayscale, or alpha. Image
decoding, circles, paths, UI controls, and a sixteen-bit rotate are outside
this module's current API.
